// AssetTokenClawbackInput: Issue #12 — on-chain clawback engine. Deliberately implemented as a standalone path, NOT on top of `transfer`: a clawback is an admin-only, legally-authorized recovery mechanism (court order, lost-key recovery, regulatory enforcement) that must be able to succeed precisely when the holder cannot or will not cooperate — for example, their address has since been suspended in compliance, or they refuse to sign. Routing this through `transfer` would force it through `from.require_auth()` and the sender-compliance check, which are exactly the two things a clawback needs to bypass. It still validates the source balance (reusing `Error::InsufficientBalance`, since the failure mode is identical to a transfer's — see the PR description for why a new error code was not added here), and emits a dedicated `clawback` event so the recovery is fully auditable on-chain. Total supply is unchanged: recovered units are credited to the administrator so every unit of supply remains backed by an account balance.
type AssetTokenClawbackInput struct {
	Admin string `json:"admin"`
	From string `json:"from"`
	Amount string `json:"amount"`
}

// AssetTokenClawbackOutput wraps the return value of asset-token::clawback.
type AssetTokenClawbackOutput struct {
	Result struct{} `json:"result"`
}

// AssetTokenUpgradeInput: Issue #11 — migrate this contract to a new WASM build without losing storage. Reuses `Error::Unauthorized` (3), already the admin-mismatch error for every other admin-gated call on this contract; no new error code is needed.
type AssetTokenUpgradeInput struct {
	Admin string `json:"admin"`
	NewWasmHash string `json:"new_wasm_hash"`
}

// AssetTokenUpgradeOutput wraps the return value of asset-token::upgrade.
type AssetTokenUpgradeOutput struct {
	Result struct{} `json:"result"`
}
